x86_64-linux  the closure type is spelled $_N or $deduced_N where clang spells it UlvE_ and its numbered forms, which [expr.prim.lambda] leaves to the implementation - the same difference every lambda case records. Two things the names do show: `tw<int>` and `tw<double>` each have a closure of their own now (_ZZ2twIiET_S0_ENK3$_0clEv beside _ZZ2twIdET_S0_ENK3$_0clEv, as clang has them), which is what the case is about; and a closure returned from a lambda is named by the function the deducing reading ran in (main, K::run) where clang nests it inside the outer closure's call operator, since that one reading builds it (A21). The runs agree, which run.sh checks.
x86_64-windows  the same: $_N and $deduced_N against clang-msvc's <lambda_N>, and the returned closures owned by main and K::run rather than nested in ??R of the outer.
arm64-darwin  the same as x86_64-linux, with Mach-O's leading underscore.
